#{[17592186168003 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n![](images/3e/3e1a6630e46ba96ca21946d9feedc452d8416fe5bac17d5e8275c41c489d19ea.svg)\nwhere ![](images/78/7853d8d4de1afb7f8899c60d7a5ea8e5ecd281594e48536c9f2d0bbef2c0301b.svg)\n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis have the form ![](images/a1/a12ab1d3c69a589aa8de66fe8d14ed19b2938a9ef5591d87860e101a95afdc55.svg) Since ![](images/3d/3db8d2103adafd2be29df482295b07ef63576be6f152793463e034e808725edf.svg) we have that ![](images/bb/bb6223675110a379d33203ff637afaa6aefd3edf507b5a93386c608aa7aafca5.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) Thus, the trajectories on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis have the form ![](images/aa/aae0f8015e0a575ca1e1fdbb37d011cbf78574649e3c9874163c3f14f1ca9929.svg) Since ![](images/23/23d0a8291d49cc1d2f0c6cff735a4d5214c2d9079dca67d803846af9b063358f.svg) we have that ![](images/69/69c464920e94e968ac5b2fe07f52d5e8a5e694f0fab6c9bfdcfd1b359a82e89e.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) Thus, the trajectories on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *stable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called a **stable node** or **sink**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw\n\n- arrows on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin), and\n\n- arrows on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory flows into the origin.\n\n![](images/11/119ac25f56cfb326a9eb39eaf63c41e8497147c80b14da3a38e557e8aeb4f905.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168003 #edb/ref 1065 14461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168003 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "98c957b9-817c-5bdd-9e7a-9680641d5c55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168003 #edb/ref 1067 "Both Negative Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168004 #edb/ref 1064 "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a decoupled linear system\n![](images/e1/e1efdd0b8c037c01450042384ce3bac663d0b549fa9557d0db09b692754cab0f.svg)\n\nRecall that a homogeneous linear system has a single equilibrium point at the origin $(0,0).$ \nTo classify this equilibrium, we look at the signs of $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2.$\n\nFirst of all, the coordinate axes are straight-line solutions:\n\n- On the $x$-axis, we have $y(t)= 0$ and $x(t)=c_1e^{\\lambda_1 t}$.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1<0,$ the $x$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1>0,$ the $x$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\n- On the $y$-axis, we have $x(t)=0$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{\\lambda_2 t}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ the $y$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ the $y$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- *Unstable node (source)* if $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2>0.$\n\n- *Stable node (sink)* if $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2<0.$ In fact, it's asymptotically stable, as the trajectories converge to the origin.\n\n- *Saddle point* if $\\lambda_1\\lambda_2<0$ (one positive, one negative).\n\nLet's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168004 #edb/ref 1065 14462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168004 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6fd81402-072b-5676-9cac-f53af20f3174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168004 #edb/ref 1067 "Classification of Equilibria" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168020 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={\\lambda }_{1}x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={\\lambda }_{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2<0$ (the opposite case, where $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2>0,$ is similar). \n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form ![](images/4d/4dc3aaf0f7dfd85070df84c13abe2cf02d85a704167569b3d3a76f2b58178c5b.svg) Since $\\lambda_1>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *away* from the origin. This is called an **unstable axis**.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form ![](images/76/76f6b9bbcdff2c87295fb0cd14dbdbbaa5e6c75eeb49c1cc158a4e8bd3da059d.svg) Since $\\lambda_2<0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *toward* the origin. This is called a **stable axis**.\n\nBecause one axis is stable and the other is unstable, the origin (equilibrium point) is called a **saddle point**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin), while\n\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory bends from the stable direction and then peels away along the unstable direction.\n\n![](images/f2/f2538a59050e604d85fd9726e688cfbd2a5c9bdff4d15dd5ed465ed04f7290b4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186168020 #edb/ref 1065 14565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168020 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8fe29b2e-2a4a-5a2d-aaea-49ed3c5a7c1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168020 #edb/ref 1067 "One Positive and One Negative Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168037 #edb/ref 1064 "In a homogeneous boundary value problem (BVP) that depends on a parameter $\\lambda$, most values of $\\lambda$ lead only to the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0$.  \n\nThe special values of $\\lambda$ that produce *nontrivial* solutions are called **eigenvalues**, and the corresponding nontrivial solutions are called **eigenfunctions**.\n\nFor example, consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0, \\quad y(0) = 0, \\quad 4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nLet's find the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. \n\nThe method for solving the differential equation depends on whether $\\lambda = 0$ or $\\lambda \\neq 0.$ To find the eigenvalues, we must therefore investigate these two possibilities as separate cases.\n\nOn the next slide, we will analyze the case where $\\lambda = 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168037 #edb/ref 1065 14646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168037 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1378993d-8bac-50d2-b1ea-4f7c3a1f5ec2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168037 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168038 #edb/ref 1064 "When the auxiliary equation has purely imaginary roots, the solutions are trigonometric, and the boundary conditions usually force $\\lambda$ to take on a discrete set of values.\nSo, we again look for values of $\\lambda$ that allow a nontrivial solution.\n\nWe will now find the **eigenvalues** and **eigenfunctions** for the boundary value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + 9\\lambda^2 y = 0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0, \\quad y(4) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 4, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nWe consider two cases based on the value of $\\lambda,$ since they correspond to different forms of the general solution.\n\n**Case 1: $\\lambda = 0$**\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation $y'' = 0.$\nIntegrating this equation twice gives the general solution:\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nTo apply the boundary conditions, we first find the derivative, $y' = A.$\n\n- Applying $y'(0) = 0$ gives $A = 0.$ The solution becomes $y = B.$\n\n- Applying $y(4) = 0$ then gives $B = 0.$\n\nThis yields the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ Since an eigenfunction must be nontrivial, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2: $\\lambda > 0$**\n\nThe auxiliary equation is $m^2 + 9\\lambda^2 = 0,$ which has roots $m = \\pm 3i\\lambda.$\nThe general solution is therefore\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3\\lambda x) + B\\sin (3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions. First, we find the derivative:\n\n$$\ny' = -3A\\lambda\\sin(3\\lambda x) + 3B\\lambda\\cos(3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\n- Applying the condition $y'(0) = 0$ yields:\n\n$$\n-3A\\lambda\\sin(0) + 3B\\lambda\\cos(0) = 0 \\quad\\implies\\quad 3B\\lambda = 0.\n$$\n\nSince we are in the case where $\\lambda > 0,$ this requires $B = 0.$ The solution simplifies to:\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\n- Next, applying the condition $y(4) = 0$ yields:\n \n\n$$\nA\\cos (12\\lambda) = 0.\n$$\n\n For a nontrivial solution, we must have $A \\neq 0,$ which means we require\n \n\n$$\n\\cos (12\\lambda) = 0.\n$$\n\n This condition is met when $12\\lambda$ is an odd multiple of $\\pi/2:$\n\n$$\n12\\lambda = \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\text{for } n = 0, 1, 2, \\dots\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\lambda$ gives the set of **eigenvalues**:\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{24}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding **eigenfunctions** are \n\n$$\ny_n(x) = A_n \\cos(3\\lambda_n x) , \\quad A_n \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168038 #edb/ref 1065 14650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168038 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2e52b01a-4f53-5d27-9984-dd731308d87d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168038 #edb/ref 1067 "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168044 #edb/ref 1064 "In the earlier topics, we saw that *symmetry* can force many Fourier coefficients to be zero automatically.\nIn particular, if a $2\\pi$-periodic function is *even*, then its Fourier series has *cosine terms only*.\n\nA **Fourier cosine series** uses this idea when a function is initially given only on the half-interval $[0,\\pi),$ but we want a full Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ (and then a $2\\pi$-periodic extension to all real $x$).\n\nTo do this, we start with a function $F(x)$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and define its **even extension** $f(x)$ to $(-\\pi,\\pi)$ by\n![](images/e2/e2e07048ac4750968947e45443f59096f395b159d4f7a846410a711194b11a14.svg)\n\nFor example, a function $F(x),$ defined on $x \\in [0,1),$ and its even extension $f(x)$ to $x \\in (-1,1)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33d92f280a5672c8d9d94553ccc1ada2a9c5ea1ff12344ca8c9aeca9f44c40c6.png)\n\nBy construction, $f$ is an *even* function, meaning $f(-x)=f(x)$. Because $f$ is even, its Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ has *cosine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is even and $\\cos(nx)$ is even for any $n\\ge 1,$ the product $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is even too. Therefore, the cosine coefficients can be computed using the *half-range formulas*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for $n\\ge 1,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo in practice, the workflow is the following:\n\n- Start with $F$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and form the even extension $f$ on $(-\\pi,\\pi).$\n\n- Compute $a_0$ and $a_n$ from the half-range integrals on $[0,\\pi).$\n\n- Write the Fourier cosine series of $f.$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168044 #edb/ref 1065 14676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e9cc2bdd-3ebe-5b87-ad23-c43cf508923b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168044 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168055 #edb/ref 1064 "Next, we consider the case $\\lambda \\neq 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions of \n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0,\n$$\n\nfor $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-18\\lambda m+81{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-9\\lambda )(m-9\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives a repeated root $m = 9\\lambda.$ The general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Applying the first boundary condition, $y(0) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\ny(0) = Ae^{0} + B(0)e^{0} = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad A = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution simplifies to $y = Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.$ Its derivative is\n\n$$\ny' = Be^{9\\lambda x} + 9B\\lambda xe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\n- Applying the second boundary condition, $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(3B{e}^{27\\lambda })+5(B{e}^{27\\lambda }+27B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ 12B{e}^{27\\lambda }+5B{e}^{27\\lambda }+135B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{27\\lambda }(17+135\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a nontrivial solution (an eigenfunction), we require $B\\neq 0.$ Since $e^{27\\lambda}$ is never zero, we must have\n\n$$\n17+135\\lambda = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad \\lambda = -\\dfrac{17}{135}.\n$$\n\nThis is our **eigenvalue**. The corresponding **eigenfunction** is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Bxe^{9(-17/135) x} = Bxe^{-17x/15}.\n$$\n\nNote that any nonzero constant multiple of this function is also an eigenfunction." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168055 #edb/ref 1065 14892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168055 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f4d9fb35-7991-5b49-bfd6-c82ad2ef1c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168055 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Trivial Solution of the Eigenvalue Problem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168056 #edb/ref 1064 "We begin by considering  the case $\\lambda = 0.$\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ in the original equation, $y'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0,$ gives a much simpler equation:\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice with respect to $x$ gives the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- The first condition, $y(0) = 0,$ implies:\n\n$$\ny(0) = A(0) + B = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad B=0.\n$$\n\nSo the solution must be of the form $y = Ax.$\n\n- For the second condition, we first find the derivative, $y' = A.$ Applying the condition $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(A\\cdot 3)+5(A) & =0 \\\\ 12A+5A & =0 \\\\ 17A & =0\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,A=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $A=0$ and $B=0,$ we are left with the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ By definition, an eigenfunction must be a *nontrivial* solution.\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue. \n\nNext, we will check the case where $\\lambda \\neq 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168056 #edb/ref 1065 14893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168056 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b3458843-3f77-548b-b991-69c3d163d75d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168056 #edb/ref 1067 "Trivial Solution of the Eigenvalue Problem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168057 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's analyze the case where the origin is a source, with ![](images/b8/b89d2dea375e40cafca8a025742ea1ba94b01ff3dd5926dc21b62444847e237f.svg)  For a decoupled system with solutions ![](images/6b/6b0666459fb6515324b663cc73f0e9327d845f40fd7772aa8850a4f14b55239d.svg) and ![](images/e7/e78f66d899f375fcf9894ab58968f92123556325139411dd278712df59c7e0ce.svg) trajectories move away from the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/3c/3cda8c604691cfc8889d64007bce911c2ea6bd3ba06a7a14cb3adef0cacbccfc.svg) then ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/27/27aba369b910849246f3c1c32106bca7e1e83a6223410a2fdc233fdea0a7367b.svg) In this case:\n![](images/b6/b6ee7ac8d56a4a4a1cdfb29948285c86491fc884911de538b875420bcabf1b55.svg)\nas ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) This means the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories move away from the origin.\n\n- If ![](images/a5/a5bd23277d5f3a652a992839592d09551cd2278773b0f92cbdc06b247f69dc64.svg) then ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/b0/b0dbb3708b2ff42502115d98637093d3b96d1f3f4ee4f9393046d0c3396068a6.svg) In this case:\n![](images/fd/fd6de4580c33c90dcadc8599e19917522b63327c668e8fec6856eec61c7ba3a3.svg)\nas ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) This means the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as the trajectories move away from the origin.\n\nNotice that in both cases, trajectories become parallel to the axis corresponding to the *dominant eigenvalue* as they move away from the origin (the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis on the left, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis on the right).\n\n![](images/28/286b72736b47e9d318f585fc0ef0ae69e4150914c3ca7a8981d236d08305a6a5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168057 #edb/ref 1065 14895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168057 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "534a4770-a0a6-5b07-a36e-0bc08f985630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168057 #edb/ref 1067 "Unstable Node (Source)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168058 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let's analyze the case where the origin is a sink, with ![](images/78/7853d8d4de1afb7f8899c60d7a5ea8e5ecd281594e48536c9f2d0bbef2c0301b.svg) For a decoupled system with solutions ![](images/6b/6b0666459fb6515324b663cc73f0e9327d845f40fd7772aa8850a4f14b55239d.svg) and ![](images/e7/e78f66d899f375fcf9894ab58968f92123556325139411dd278712df59c7e0ce.svg) trajectories approach the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/48/481eb665d12add01a80a4646ec2762c89c9a543bf87b959b246d469a450eca6d.svg) then ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/27/27aba369b910849246f3c1c32106bca7e1e83a6223410a2fdc233fdea0a7367b.svg) As ![](images/37/370259707c15937e566c781bb8f7aaf39643a5c059b246f2cec5477528eee994.svg)\n![](images/b6/b6ee7ac8d56a4a4a1cdfb29948285c86491fc884911de538b875420bcabf1b55.svg)\nThis means the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as trajectories approach the origin.\n\n- If ![](images/80/80105ab4e61c0e2a59d11da4b534753b3c811b424f1e0b82704c6e28faecf9b8.svg) then ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/b0/b0dbb3708b2ff42502115d98637093d3b96d1f3f4ee4f9393046d0c3396068a6.svg) As ![](images/37/370259707c15937e566c781bb8f7aaf39643a5c059b246f2cec5477528eee994.svg)\n![](images/fd/fd6de4580c33c90dcadc8599e19917522b63327c668e8fec6856eec61c7ba3a3.svg)\nThis means the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as trajectories approach the origin.\n\nNotice that in both cases, trajectories become parallel to the axis corresponding to the *dominant eigenvalue* as they move toward the origin (the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis on the left, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis on the right).\n\n![](images/b6/b665d5328ae362ada2208da2583fd90247078490145426072455d43f8d36227f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168058 #edb/ref 1065 14896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168058 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3b95d431-e3ab-5635-b641-a019d23397b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168058 #edb/ref 1067 "Stable Node (Sink)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168149 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the average value of a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $D = [a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{ave} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Length}(D)}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThere is an analogous definition for functions of two variables, stated below:\n\n*Suppose $D$ is a closed and bounded region in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ which has non-zero finite area $\\textrm{Area}(D).$ If $f (x, y)$ is a continuous function on $D,$ then **the average value of $\\boldsymbol f$ on $\\boldsymbol D$** is given by*\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} =\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168149 #edb/ref 1065 7770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168149 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c862ff42-4e5b-5bbb-aed6-3d9760d339b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168149 #edb/ref 1067 "The Average Value of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168150 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $[a, b],$ the mean value theorem for integrals guarantees that there exists a number $c \\in [a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a),\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0afb9b15787eb89f73f322290bdbe2288027c54924d274c438773ce151a17291.png)\n\n \n\nWe have a similar theorem for functions with two variables. The so-called **mean value theorem for double integrals** states:\n\n*If the function $f(x,y)$ is continuous on a closed and bounded region $D,$ then there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nThis has a nice geometrical interpretation when $f$ is non-negative on $D.$ In this case, the theorem tells us that there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ \n\nThe diagram below shows the case when $D$ is rectangular, and the corresponding cylinder is a rectangular solid.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed477207ee61fbdaf963ed8a127e3f1d4cf76707d2eeb4b450fd49f78e15b7e.png)\n\n \n\nIn general, the point $(a,b)$ mentioned in the theorem is not unique. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168150 #edb/ref 1065 7778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168150 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b65eeb5f-8a44-55b9-819c-1c6c0d979d1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168150 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168151 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the continuous function \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x+3y^2+2\n$$\n\nand the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[xy+{y}^{3}+2y]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}+12x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, our mean-value equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =f(a,b)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 7 & =f(a,b) \\\\ 7 & =a+3{b}^{2}+2 \\\\ a & =5-3{b}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation describes part of a parabola in the $xy$-plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46873c4ecb290380488e589082fc0759324d3540494f5b83e2d64db5d9cdf62a.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find a particular point that satisfies the mean value theorem, we can set, for example, $b=1 \\in [0,2]$ and obtain $a=2.$ So, the point $(2,1)$ satisfies the mean value theorem for double integrals. Consequently, the two solids below must have the same volume.\n\n![](images/82/82d3a762ab323339570e6010038e75ce95f3fdbde958a2ffa876972cbf91e8fb.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168151 #edb/ref 1065 7784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168151 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b890c915-3364-5ac3-b88b-298ed92d9def" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168151 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172057 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172057 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172057 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172057 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172057 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172057 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172057 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172057 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172057 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Parametrization of an Ellipsoid Using Spherical Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172057 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172057 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "feb953f4-0a88-5d41-b62d-e8d7acd05801" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172058 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172058 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172058 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172058 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172058 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172058 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172058 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172058 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 1