 [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e70dec8f-9828-5fe7-bdb7-47e6557fc889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1045 "Computing the Singular Values of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186184968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d3825ee6-b390-5321-ae3c-5fb0645c7b7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184968 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184968 #edb/ref 1061 30545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184968 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f6870e18-6c9b-57a7-81ca-e24f2af2e69d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184968 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184970 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184970 #edb/ref 1061 30988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184970 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "92a4b43a-dde9-5e42-8877-3e32ba9082f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184970 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184971 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184971 #edb/ref 1061 30989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184971 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "551770c2-906e-5338-b743-2d2902903e74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184971 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184972 #edb/ref 1061 30990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184972 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "749703b1-407a-5adb-8fda-1176ec5284b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184972 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the singular values of a real matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nAlso, since $A^T \\! A$ is a positive semi-definite matrix, the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A$ must be always non-negative real numbers.\n\nLet's compute the singular values of the matrix $A,$ given by \n\n![](images/a9/a9edbe1fee59758c9557d95ea8f62468f9f3cb98449c437a274bdbf5c30f7dc6.svg)\n\nNow, since\n\n- $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix, and\n\n- $A^T$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\nthen $A^T \\! A$  must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 3}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 3} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nLet's now compute our singular values. First, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When listing the singular values of a matrix, we usually write them in descending order.\n\n- If $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, then $A^T A$ is a $n\\times n$ positive semi-definite matrix. This means that $A$ has *at most* $n$ distinct singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1065 4891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6230f35e-b526-5e84-be30-86c8cdcbd301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186186402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186186403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186186402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186186404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186186402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186186405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186186402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186186406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186186402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186186407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186186402 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186186402 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186186402 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186186403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186186402 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8b741eef-9c5a-588c-9901-de8a736211a9" #edb/ref 1759218